CAT = "auwahHarness"; SEG = "HAR"
Q = [
('What is the maximum free fall distance a harness-based fall arrest system should allow?',
 '2 metres', ['1 metre', '3 metres', '4 metres'],
 'Harness fall arrest systems should be set up so a person free falls no more than 2 metres before the system takes effect. Less is better.'),
('What minimum rating is commonly required for a single-person fall arrest anchorage?',
 '15 kN', ['2 kN', '5 kN', '50 kN'],
 'AS/NZS 1891.4 sets the anchor strength; for one person this is commonly 15 kN. It is often hard to achieve on a part-built timber roof.'),
('Why should friction or chemical insert anchors not be used where the load is a direct pull-out?',
 "Their grip can't be seen", ['They are too heavy to carry', 'They rust within weeks', 'They need a crane to fit'],
 'Insert-type anchors in partial-depth holes should not take direct pull-out fall loads. Their grip below the surface can only be confirmed by pull-out testing.'),
('When should you connect your lanyard to the anchorage point?',
 'Before reaching any fall risk', ['Once you reach the roof edge', 'Only when the wind picks up', 'After you start the task'],
 'Anchorages should be placed so you can clip on before you move into any position where a fall is possible, and stay connected until you are back in a safe area.'),
('A harness and lanyard have just arrested a fall. What should happen to them?',
 'Withdraw them for inspection', ['Reuse them for the rest of the day', 'Wash them and put them away', 'Use them only for restraint'],
 'Equipment that has arrested a fall must not be used again until it has been inspected and certified safe by a competent person. Energy absorbers are often single use.'),
('When should a competent person inspect fall arrest components?',
 'Before use, regularly, after a fall', ['Only when the harness is first bought', 'Only once every twelve months', 'Only when the user asks for it'],
 'Each component should be inspected after it is installed and before use, at regular intervals, and immediately after it has arrested a fall.'),
('Why is a harness fall arrest system often unsuitable on a single-storey house?',
 'Not enough clearance below', ['The roof is too strong', 'Harnesses are banned on houses', 'The anchors are too high'],
 'A fall arrest system needs enough height to deploy fully, which the housing code says can be 6.5 metres depending on the system. On a low roof the worker may hit the ground first.'),
('Which of these adds to the total fall distance in a fall arrest system?',
 'Energy absorber deployment', ['Using a shorter lanyard', 'Raising the anchor point', 'Removing slack from the lanyard'],
 "Total fall distance includes free fall, lanyard and lifeline stretch, slack and the energy absorber extending, plus the worker's height. Shorter lanyards and higher anchors reduce it."),
('Where should your fall arrest anchor point be compared with where you work?',
 'As high as possible', ['Level with your feet', 'At about knee height', 'At ground level nearby'],
 'Working above the anchor increases free fall, impact force and the chance of the lanyard snagging. Keep the anchor as high as the equipment permits.'),
('Why should a lanyard not be used together with an inertia reel?',
 'It can add excessive free fall', ['It makes the reel lock too soon', 'It doubles the anchor strength', 'It shortens the harness life'],
 'Combining a lanyard with an inertia reel can let a worker fall too far before the fall is arrested. Use each device as the maker intends.'),
('Why may an inertia reel fail to lock on a steep roof?',
 'A slow slide may not trigger it', ['Steel roof sheets block its signal', 'Reels only work on flat roofs', 'The line is too short there'],
 'Inertia reels lock on a sudden pull. A worker sliding down a steeply pitched roof may pull the line out slowly, so the reel keeps paying out.'),
('How should an inertia reel be used during normal work?',
 'To arrest falls, not to hang on', ['As a seat to rest in between tasks', 'As a hoist for lifting tools', 'As a support while leaning out'],
 'Inertia reels are not designed for continuous support, so do not lock them and hang off them while working. They are there to arrest a fall.'),
('How many people may be attached to one inertia reel line?',
 'One', ['Two', 'Three', 'As many as fit'],
 'Only one person may be attached to one line. A second user overloads the system and interferes with its locking.'),
('What type of snap hook should a fall arrest system use?',
 'Double action', ['Single action', 'Spring gate only', 'Screwless hook'],
 'Snap hooks and karabiners should need at least two distinct, deliberate actions to open, which stops them opening by accident.'),
("What is 'roll-out' in a fall arrest system?",
 'A connector releasing itself', ['A harness strap slipping loose', 'An anchor pulling from a roof', 'A reel line jamming'],
 'Roll-out happens when a hook or karabiner presses against an incompatible fitting and opens by itself. Check components are compatible, even from the same brand.'),
('Why should you never clip one snap hook directly onto another?',
 'It can cause roll-out', ['It doubles the free fall', 'It makes the line too short', 'It voids the harness rating'],
 'Snap hooks connected to each other can prevent the hook working safely and cause roll-out. Connect only to rated anchorages and fittings.'),
('A worker anchored well off to one side falls over a roof edge, and the line slides along the edge until it hangs straight down. What is this called?',
 'Swing down', ['Roll-out', 'Free fall', 'Suspension intolerance'],
 'Swing down is the pendulum effect when the line slides along the edge until vertical, so the worker may hit the ground or the line may cut. Swing back is hitting the structure.'),
('Which measure reduces the risk of a pendulum swing in a fall?',
 'An anchor square to the edge', ['A longer lanyard to the anchor', 'A lower anchor point on the roof', 'Working faster near the edge'],
 'Placing the anchorage at right angles to where the line crosses the edge, adding intermediate anchors, or fitting guardrails all reduce swing down and swing back.'),
('Why is a sharp roof or beam edge a hazard to fall arrest lines?',
 'The line can be cut in a fall', ['It makes the line too tight', 'It wears down your boot soles', 'It speeds up the inertia reel'],
 'Lines loaded over a sharp concrete or steel edge can fail. Use lines tested for edge use or protect the edge.'),
('How does a restraint technique protect a worker?',
 'It stops them reaching the edge', ['It catches them once they fall off', 'It slows their descent', 'It holds them at the edge'],
 'Restraint uses a harness and lanyard, fixed to an anchor or line, set so the wearer physically cannot reach a position where they could fall.'),
('Above what roof slope is a restraint technique generally not suitable?',
 '15 degrees', ['5 degrees', '26 degrees', '35 degrees'],
 'The code lists a slope over 15 degrees as a situation where restraint may not be reasonably practicable, as footing is hard to keep without tensioning the line.'),
('Why is a restraint technique unsuitable on a fragile roof?',
 'You could fall through it', ['The anchors are too strong', 'It restricts your view', 'Fragile roofs are flat'],
 'Restraint only keeps you from the edge. If the surface can give way, you could fall through it, so a different control is needed.'),
('What load should an anchorage used for a restraint technique be designed for?',
 'Fall arrest loading', ['Body weight only', "Half the worker's weight", 'The weight of the lanyard'],
 'Restraint anchorages should be designed for fall arrest loads in case the system is misused or the worker slips into a fall.'),
('Which harness should be worn for fall arrest?',
 'A well-fitted full body harness', ['A waist belt and short lanyard', 'A full body harness worn loose', 'A sit harness with a waist belt'],
 'A full body harness spreads arrest forces across the body. It must be correctly fitted, as a loose harness can injure the wearer or let them slip out.'),
('On a vertical anchorage line, where should the fall arrest device attach to the harness?',
 'The frontal attachment point', ['The side hip D-ring', 'The rear leg strap buckle', 'The tool belt loop at the back'],
 "For vertical lines and rails the locking device should attach to the harness's frontal (chest) attachment with a short connection, no more than 300 mm."),
('With a vertical anchorage line on a ladder, what is the maximum free fall that should be allowed?',
 '600 mm', ['300 mm', '1 metre', '2 metres'],
 'Vertical anchorage line systems should limit free fall to a maximum of 600 mm, with a lanyard assembly no longer than 300 mm.'),
('How does a double (Y) lanyard protect a worker climbing a structure?',
 'One tail always stays connected', ['Both tails stay unclipped while moving', 'It doubles the free fall', 'It replaces the harness'],
 'With a double lanyard you clip the next tail on before unclipping the last, so you are never disconnected. Do not back-hook it or pass it between your legs.'),
('During a pre-use harness check, which finding means it must not be used?',
 'Frayed webbing near a buckle', ['Dust on the shoulder straps only', 'A name written on the label', 'Slight fading of the colour'],
 'Cuts, fraying, burns, chemical damage, broken stitching or damaged hardware mean the harness is taken out of service. Also check the label and use-by date.'),
('Which harness detail should you check before use besides the webbing?',
 "That it's within its use-by date", ['That it matches your hi-vis colour', 'That it was bought locally', 'That it is the newest model'],
 'Check the label, use-by or retirement date and inspection record, as well as the webbing, stitching, buckles and D-rings.'),
('Besides a harness, what PPE should a worker using fall arrest wear?',
 'A helmet', ['Earplugs', 'A dust mask', 'Knee pads'],
 'Workers using fall arrest should wear suitable head protection, as a fall or swing back can strike the head on the structure.'),
('What does an energy absorber in a lanyard do?',
 'Softens the force of an arrest', ['Stops you reaching the edge', 'Locks the line automatically', 'Shortens the lanyard as you work'],
 'An energy absorber tears or extends during an arrest, lowering the force on the body and the anchorage. Its extension must be counted in the fall clearance.'),
('Why should a worker never use a fall arrest system alone on site?',
 'No one could rescue them', ["The system won't lock", 'It voids the lanyard warranty', 'The anchor needs a helper'],
 'There should be at least one other person on site who can rescue the worker promptly, because hanging in a harness quickly becomes dangerous.'),
]
